<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Travancore</h1><p class="st-meta">1729 CE – 1949 CE · capital Thiruvananthapuram</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of the southern tip of India, built by Marthanda Varma and known for its temple-state ideology, high literacy and social reform.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marthanda-varma">Marthanda Varma</a>, ruler of the small chiefdom of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-venad">Venad</a> from 1729, crushed rival nobles, annexed neighbouring principalities and defeated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-colachel">Colachel</a> in 1741. In 1750 he dedicated the enlarged kingdom to the deity Padmanabha, ruling thereafter as the god's servant from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thiruvananthapuram">Thiruvananthapuram</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Travancore allied with the British against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> and became a subsidiary ally in 1795 and 1805; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velu-thampi-dalawa">Velu Thampi</a>'s revolt of 1809 was suppressed. Rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swathi-thirunal">Swathi Thirunal</a> patronised music and learning, and the state invested early in schools, so that literacy rose far above the Indian average. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-entry-proclamation">Temple Entry Proclamation</a> of 1936 opened state temples to all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>. Its economy rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, coconut and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1947 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">diwan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/c-p-ramaswami-iyer">C. P. Ramaswami Iyer</a> proposed independence, but after protests and an attack on him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. On 1 July 1949 Travancore merged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a> to form <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/travancore-cochin">Travancore-Cochin</a>, the core of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> from 1956.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/travancore.jpg" alt="Travancore" width="320" height="182"><figcaption>Image: Washiucho · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kingdom_of_Travancore.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1729 CE – 1949 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Thiruvananthapuram</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Marthanda Varma, Swathi Thirunal, Chithira Thirunal Balarama Varma</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism; large Christian and Muslim minorities</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malayalam, Tamil</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Travancore, Thiruvithamkoor</dd></dl></aside></div>
